Project Overview
Climate change — in part driven by human activity, including AI projects like this — is transforming the climatic conditions of the Andes in southern Chile.

This project seeks to answer:

How will changing temperatures affect the habitable range of vegetation?

Will native plants suffer due to increased environmental stress, shrinking their range? Or could warming conditions expand this range, as new species colonize higher elevations?

Glacial Retreat & Soil Formation
Warming is also driving glacial retreat, revealing fresh, barren rock. Over time, soil forms in these new areas — a process indicated by the growth of complex vegetation.

By monitoring vegetation's spatial extent, we can proxy soil formation and identify the timeline of ecological succession, from shrub to tree.

Remote Sensing with Sentinel-2
Vegetation health and presence can be tracked using Sentinel-2 satellite imagery, specifically through the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI).

NDVI Formula
